{"agent_id":"anon-a55dfdea","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-09-27T14:20:46.042233802Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"12547cd9-b5aa-4555-a547-2ca714ed6dda","comment_uuid":"fc9c44f8-3bee-4a09-b0dd-8f28b97086fe","author":"anon-a55dfdea","content":"WHY: The seven-phase Kernel-0 assurance continuation is complete through bounded real-I/O storage-cut testing; the results are now reviewable on the authorized branch.\nWHAT: A1–A7 results and the frozen review packet/index are published. A7 observed actual SQLite positional writes, fdatasync, and journal unlink, then killed the holder at 90 before/after I/O points; all 29 non-final cuts per operation recovered the old endpoint and each final journal-removal cut recovered the new endpoint. Twelve deliberate split-write cases recovered the old endpoint; raw bytes differed from both endpoints in 10/12. A1’s strongest counterexample remains same-root stale database restoration after acknowledged revocation, which can resurrect authority and is outside the declared storage-failure boundary. A2 also confirms that lost sink acknowledgement plus retry can duplicate an irreversible effect. These are profile/boundary findings; no Kernel-0 semantic change or new primitive was justified. The final continuation points at review index commit ef180ef28ee692a845507a4ace02bd3fa1f10d18. Final cursor commit fef63c4d16423b089207b774c5ad1f6435bb6b19 is on codex/kernel-0-reasoning-566, and the remote branch ref was verified equal to that full SHA.\nHOW CERTAIN: Evidence-based for the bounded models, executable histories, and tested Linux x86-64/glibc 2.35, Python 3.10.12, SQLite 3.37.2, GCC 11.4.0 runtime. The realization experiment uses an LD_PRELOAD interposer at actual pwrite64/fdatasync/unlink calls and SIGKILL; it is not a filesystem or SQLite proof.\nWHAT NOT TESTED: No power loss, hardware torn-sector behavior, failing sync device, arbitrary VFS, malicious rollback, or independent adversarial review. The same-root restore demonstrates the currentness gap; preventing it needs a trustworthy non-rollback freshness anchor or an explicit narrower storage premise. Next action: independent adversarial review against the frozen packet.","created_at":"2026-09-27T14:20:46.042164946Z","kind":"result"}}
